# A Data‐Driven Approach to Molten Salt Synthesis of N‐Rich Carbon Adsorbents for Selective CO2 Capture

## abstract
Applying a design of experiments methodology to the molten salt synthesis of nanoporous carbons enables inverse design and optimization of nitrogen (N)-rich carbon adsorbents with excellent CO2 /N2 selectivity and appreciable CO2 capacity for carbon capture via swing adsorption from dilute gas mixtures such as natural gas combined cycle flue gas. This data-driven study reveals fundamental structure-function relationships between the synthesis conditions, physicochemical properties, and achievable selective adsorption performance of N-rich nanoporous carbons derived from molten salt synthesis for CO2 capture. Taking advantage of size-sieving separation of CO2 (3.30 Å) from N2 (3.64 Å) within the turbostratic nanostructure of these N-rich carbons, while limiting deleterious N2 adsorption in a weaker adsorption site that harms selectivity, enables a large CO2 capacity (0.73 mmol g-1 at 30.4 Torr and 30 °C) with noteworthy concurrent CO2 /N2 selectivity as predicted by the ideal adsorbed solution theory (SIAST = 246) with an adsorbed phase purity of 91% from a simulated gas stream containing only 4% CO2 . Optimized N-rich porous carbons, with good physicochemical stability, low cost, and moderate regeneration energy, can achieve performance for selective CO2 adsorption that competes with other classes of advanced porous materials such as chemisorbing zeolites and functionalized metal-organic frameworks.

A Data-Driven Approach to Molten Salt Synthesis of N-Rich Carbon Adsorbents for Selective $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ Capture
Applying a design of experiments methodology to the molten salt synthesis of nanoporous carbons enables inverse design and optimization of nitrogen (N)-rich carbon adsorbents with excellent $\mathsf{c o}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity and appreciable $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ capacity for carbon capture via swing adsorption from dilute gas mixtures such as natural gas combined cycle flue gas. This data-driven study reveals fundamental structure-function relationships between the synthesis conditions, physicochemical properties, and achievable selective adsorption performance of N-rich nanoporous carbons derived from molten salt synthesis for $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ capture. Taking advantage of size-sieving separation of $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ $(3.30\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ from $\ensuremath{\mathsf{N}}_{2}$ (3.64 Å) within the turbostratic nanostructure of these N-rich carbons, while limiting deleterious $\ensuremath{\mathsf{N}}_{2}$ adsorption in a weaker adsorption site that harms selectivity, enables a large $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ capacity $\left(0.73\mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1}}\right.$ at 30.4 Torr and $30~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ with noteworthy concurrent $\mathsf{c o}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity as predicted by the ideal adsorbed solution theory $(S_{\mathrm{\tiny{IAST}}}=246)$ with an adsorbed phase purity of $91\%$ from a simulated gas stream containing only $4\%\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ . Optimized N-rich porous carbons, with good physicochemical stability, low cost, and moderate regeneration energy, can achieve performance for selective $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ adsorption that competes with other classes of advanced porous materials such as chemisorbing zeolites and functionalized metal-organic frameworks.
## 1. Introduction
Advanced nanoporous carbons, exhibiting a promising combination of low cost, good chemical stability, and widely tunable properties, have received considerable attention for potential applications for sustainability ranging from renewable energy storage to catalysis.[1–5] Recently, our group identified the propensity for a subset of these porous carbons (viz., nitrogen (N)- rich, turbostratic hard carbons) to enable carbon capture via the size-sieving selective adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from mixed gas streams composed primarily of molecular $\Nu_{2}$ , such as those encountered in post-combustion flue gas.[6,7] Compared to other classes of adsorbents under consideration for carbon capture, porous carbons feature benefits in stability and ease of regeneration but have historically suffered from a lack of tunability and insufficient selectivity due to nonspecific mechanisms of physisorption.[8,9]
One of the most common methods for the synthesis of porous carbons, “chemical activation,” is a process in which organic precursors are treated with an oxidizing salt at high temperatures $(\approx700-$ $1200+^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ in inert atmospheres to texturize and exfoliate the materials, inducing abundant (micro)porosity.[10,11] The presence of N heteroatoms during nanocarbon synthesis can shift the synthetic mechanism away from traditional “intercalative-exfoliation,” which relies on the carbothermal reduction of alkali cations and results in disordered porous carbons with large surface areas $(>2000~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ .[12,13] Instead, with sufficient $\mathrm{~N~}$ content in the precursors, synthesis proceeded through “molten salt templating,” a synthetic mechanism that encouraged semi-crystallinity and an N-rich surface chemistry.[10] N-rich nanocarbons synthesized through molten salt templating exhibited smaller d-spacings and $\Nu_{2}$ -accessible surface areas, resulting in highly selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, even from dilute streams.[6,7]
During molten salt templating, often dubbed “ionothermal synthesis,” molten inorganic salts act as high-temperature ionic liquid solvents with low vapor pressures.[14–18] The presence of molten salts modulates pyrolysis and carbonization of organic precursors by enabling liquid reaction media for enhanced transport and overall precursor lability, significantly modifying the thermodynamic landscape of accessible carbon nanostructures as compared to solid-state pyrolysis/activation. Additionally, salt clusters, which are easily washed away after pyrolysis, can act as hard template porogens around which precursors carbonize, effectively inducing porosity after removing the salt templates via aqueous washing. As applied to carbon materials, molten salt synthesis has shown success in tuning nanocarbons for renewable energy storage (e.g., as active materials for batteries and supercapacitors) and electrocatalysis (e.g., as metal-free catalysts or supports) but has been relatively less explored for selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption and carbon capture.[15]
Perhaps the most significant hurdle toward precision synthesis of carbon nanomaterials lies in the complex relationship between multivariable synthesis conditions and the physicochemical properties of the resulting carbons. Commonly, mechanisms of nanocarbon synthesis are studied empirically by changing one variable at a time. While this approach is effective in identifying how a desired response trends with a given synthetic variable of interest, any multivariate effects are unfortunately overlooked. In fact, in systems where the influences of multiple parameters on a given response are correlated, costly and time-consuming onevariable-at-a-time approaches often fail to identify the global optimum response over the entire parameter space. A recent contribution by Cao et al. thoughtfully outlines this dilemma and suggests that careful experimental design can enable global, instead of local, optimization of synthetic conditions.[19] Such an approach, based on the classical “design of experiments” methodology, has recently shown success in the synthesis of complex nanomaterials but has yet to be applied to nanocarbon synthesis mediated by molten salts.[20–24]
For molten salt synthesis of carbon nanomaterials, synthetic parameters of importance include the identity of organic precursors and salts; the salt:precursor ratio; and the thermal profile employed during synthesis. Each of these factors is hypothesized to uniquely impact the properties of the resulting carbon materials. For instance, precursor $\mathrm{~N~}$ content alters mechanisms of synthesis and the resulting physicochemical properties of carbon adsorbents.[7,10,12] Additionally, the lattice energy, melting point, and charge density (i.e., Lewis acidity) of the molten salt solvent can strongly influence both surface chemistry and porosity of nanocarbons.[15,18] For example, Liu and Antonietti elegantly demonstrated that molten salts can be employed to efficiently control chemical activation by varying the identity of reactive oxysalts in the precursor mixture,[17,25] and Pampel and Fellinger showed that varying the molar ratio of $\mathrm{Na^{+}}$ to $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ cations in a mixture strongly influences pore structure via templating by phase-separated solid salt nanoclusters.[26] Our group and others have also shown that salt ratio plays a critical role by modulating solvation, stabilization, structural reorganization, and pore templating of carbon nanostructures.[27–29] Furthermore, the synthetic temperature profile imparts a significant influence on the resulting chemistry and nanomorphology by regulating chemical reactivity and crystal growth due to differences in the (meta)stability of different chemical functionalities/phases. In the face of such a vast parameter space where significant interactions between synthetic variables of interest are expected, efficient choice of experimentation can enable response surface mapping via the classical design of experiments methodology[30,31] to 1) understand complex, multivariate syntheses processes and 2) optimize conditions to obtain a carbon with desired physical/chemical properties.
In the following, we detail structural/chemical investigation of the resulting material properties (surface area/porosity, surface chemistry, semi-crystallinity) and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption characteristics (capacity, thermodynamics, and selectivity) with physisorptive and X-ray techniques. Further, by fitting the results to empirical, second-order multivariate models accounting for interactions between independent variables via multiple regression with step-wise regularization, we mapped various responses (i.e., material properties, adsorption performance) as a function of the four synthetic variables of interest. More information about regression and model reduction can be found in Section S2, Supporting Information. This study enables the inverse design of advanced nanocarbon adsorbents and helps define general synthesis-structure-function relations of selective carbon physisorbents for carbon capture from dilute post-combustion flue gas.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Characterizing Material Properties
Complete details regarding the preparation of precursor mixtures, synthetic protocols, and material characterization can be found in the Supporting Information. After successful synthesis and washing of inorganic salt byproducts, the surface chemistry was quantified via deconvolution of X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS). As shown in Figure S4, Supporting Information, survey scans indicated the presence of C, N, and O elements as expected—only trace salt impurities ( $\cdot{<}1$ $a t\%$ of $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaNa^{+}}$ , and/or $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ ) or other contaminants were detected on the surfaces of the resulting carbons. Integrating the 1s regions of C, N, and O elements revealed the relative abundance of N heteroatoms in the nearsurface region, and these results are summarized in Figure 2a and Figures S6–S13 and Table S8, Supporting Information. The surface composition of the sample set varied depending on the synthetic condition, with C contents ranging from ${\approx}67$ to $84\%$ ; N contents from ${\approx}12$ to $26\mathrm{at\%}$ ; and O contents from ${\approx}5$ to $8\%$ .
In general, while O content did not vary much, the N content (and $\mathsf{N}/\mathsf{C}$ ratio) was observed to decrease significantly with temperature and to increase slightly with the melamine weight percent in the organic precursors. The $\mathrm{~N~}$ content was less sensitive to the salt:carbon ratio and/or cooling rate. This observed temperature-dependence points toward the thermal stability of different surface N motifs, as discussed in our previous work.[10] Given sufficient N content in the precursor, the temperature of synthesis ostensibly set an upper bound for the amount of $\mathrm{~N~}$ retention in the resulting nanocarbons in this molten salt-mediated synthesis process. This contrasts with other commonly-employed schemes for nanocarbon synthesis, such as chemical activation, where oxidizing salts can react directly with and remove additional N motifs from the carbon surface.
More detailed analysis of the distribution of the total N content into these different motifs was achieved by fitting high-resolution scans of the N 1s region with four Gaussian–Lorentzian peaks, each corresponding to unique N surface functionalities (viz. pyridinic, or $\mathrm{C-N=C}$ , at $398.1\pm0.05\mathrm{eV};$ pyrrolic, or $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{NH}$ , at 399.5 $\pm\:0.09\:\mathrm{eV};$ graphitic, or $\mathsf{N C}_{3}$ at $400.8\pm0.09\ \mathrm{eV};$ and oxidized N species, or $\mathrm{NO}_{x}$ , at $402.5\pm0.11\mathrm{eV}_{\l}$ and can be found in Table S8 and Figures S8 and S9, Supporting Information. The decrease in $\mathrm{~N~}$ content with temperature is largely attributed to a loss of $\mathrm{C-N=C}$ species, which decreased from ${\approx}14$ to $5a t\%$ between 700 and $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , regardless of the $\mathrm{~N~}$ content in the semi-carbonized precursor. On the other hand, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{NH}$ and $\mathsf{N C}_{3}$ species both decreased only from ${\approx}4$ to $2.5\mathrm{at\%}$ over the same range of synthetic temperatures. As a result, in addition to a decrease in absolute N content, higher synthetic temperatures were also associated with a smaller proportion of $\mathrm{C-N=C}$ functionalities. In sum, the surface chemistry of these carbons was sensitive to the N content of the precursors and largely dependent on the temperature of synthesis. The salt:carbon ratio and the cooling rate influenced the resulting N content to only a small extent.
On the other hand, unlike the $\mathrm{~N~}$ content, the porosity and surface area of the resulting nanocarbons were significantly impacted by the salt:carbon ratio. At $77~\mathrm{K},$ these carbons displayed $\Nu_{2}$ isotherms with mixed Type $\mathrm{I/IV}$ character and H4 hysteresis loops, indicating a hierarchical distribution of micro $(<2\ \mathrm{nm})$ and mesoporosity $(2-50\ \mathrm{nm})$ (Figures S14–S19, Supporting Information).[34,35] Notably, clear micropore filling at low relative pressures followed by continuous multilayer adsorption at larger relative pressures $(p/p_{0}>0.1)$ was observed. Additionally, the desorption branch exhibited open hysteresis for some of the least-porous carbon samples, pointing toward the He entrapment effect in ultramicropores with critical dimensions below $0.5\mathrm{nm}$ .[36] Fitting the adsorption branch of the $\Nu_{2}$ isotherms with kernel-based regression enabled extraction of pore size distributions according to quenched solid density functional theory (QSDFT).[37] These pore size distributions (Figures S20–S22, Supporting Information) revealed that the total pore volume varied from ${\approx}0.1$ to $0.2\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for these carbons. We note here that any pores inaccessible to (i.e., smaller than the kinetic diameter of ) the $\Nu_{2}$ probe molecules will not be observed in these adsorption isotherms, and as such, a significant volume of nanoporosity could be underestimated via analysis of $\Nu_{2}$ physisorption alone. This consideration is of critical importance for the investigation of adsorbents for carbon capture, as the slightly smaller kinetic diameter of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (3.30 vs 3.64 Å) grants access to some quantity of “hidden,” or undercounted, nanoporosity.
In general, the $(\Nu_{2}$ -accessible) porosity seemed to increase slightly with $\mathrm{~N~}$ content in the precursor and with synthetic temperature but to decrease with increased salt:carbon ratios. For a majority of the samples, approximately $50\%$ of the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ - accessible porosity was derived from micropores with diameters ${<}2~\mathrm{nm}$ ; only at the lowest synthetic temperatures did a majority of the porosity stem from larger mesopores (Figure S23, Supporting Information). Additionally, the microporosity varied over a larger range than the mesoporosity (Figure S24, Supporting Information). Multi-point Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) analysis of these carbons indicated $\Nu_{2}$ -accessible surface areas ranging from ${\approx}100$ to $400\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , as shown in Figure 2b, and the surface area was strongly correlated with the microporosity (Figures S25 and S26, Supporting Information). While the relationships between the synthetic variables and the resulting porosity are complex, in general, the samples with the smallest BET surface areas were synthesized at low temperatures, with small N contents in the precursor, and/or with large salt:carbon ratios.
Additionally, powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) was employed to quantify the fringe parameters of these turbostratic carbons, which are known to display characteristic Bragg peaks near ${\approx}25^{\circ}$ and $45^{\circ}\ 2\Theta$ associated with the 002 and 101 reflections of the turbostratic structure, respectively.[38] Fitting the observed XRD spectra with Lorentzian peaks revealed the average apparent d-spacings associated with interlayer spacing $\left(\mathrm{d}_{002}\right)$ and in-plane order $\left(\mathbf{d}_{101}\right)$ (Figures S27–S29, Supporting Information). A Scherrer analysis enabled an estimation of the average coherence length, both along the in-plane, 101 axis $\left(L_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ and the out-of-plane, 002 axis $\left(\boldsymbol{L}_{\mathrm{c}}\right)$ . For these materials, the $L_{\mathrm{a}}$ parameter quantifies how defective the individual sheets of turbostratic carbon are; larger $L_{\mathrm{a}}$ values correspond to, on average, less defective sheets with larger coherent domains.[39] These defects could include pores, heteroatoms, and/or structure terminations that cause deviation in the hexagonal structure of the typical $\mathrm{C}_{6}$ ring. In fact, the $L_{\mathrm{a}}$ parameter is directly related to the commonly reported $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ ratio from Raman analysis of carbon materials.[40] The $L_{\mathrm{c}}$ parameter, on the other hand, quantifies how many of these defective turbostratic domains are stacked atop one another; at a given interlayer d-spacing, larger $L_{\mathrm{c}}$ values correspond to a greater number of stacked “sheets” (on average). Figure 2c,d displays the observed $\mathrm{d}_{002}$ and $L_{\mathrm{a}}$ values, respectively. For these carbons, the interlayer d-spacing varied only slightly from ${\approx}3.4$ to $3.6\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ . Critically, our previous work has implicated that this range of dspacing is required for the size-sieving adsorptive separation of $\bar{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}(3.30\bar{\mathrm{A}})$ from $\Nu_{2}$ (3.64 Å).[6,7] On the other hand, the in-plane coherence length (i.e., $\left.L_{\mathrm{a}}\right\rangle$ varied more significantly, from ${\approx}2$ to 10 Å. Interestingly, both of these fringe parameters, together quantifying the turbostratic nanostructure, were strongly correlated to the final N content of the carbons.
Figure 3 graphically displays the relationships between the final N content, surface area, and fringe parameters of the resulting turbostratic carbons. As discussed previously, and in contrast to the final N content (which was dependent mainly on the synthetic temperature and the precursor N content), the surface area was sensitive to the presence of the molten salt template. As a result, Figure 3a shows an apparent lack of correlation between the porosity and the surface chemistry of these carbons. This observation is rare for porous carbons; typically, the synthesis of nanocarbons, especially via chemical activation, results in a tradeoff between porosity and heteroatom-rich surface chemistry. By decoupling the mechanisms of N-retention and porosity generation, molten salt synthesis therefore enables independent tuning of carbon surface chemistry and adsorbent porous architecture (Table S9, Supporting Information).
In line with our previous results, both the interlayer dspacing $\left(\mathrm{d}_{002}\right)$ and the lateral crystallite coherence $\left(L_{\mathrm{a}}\right)$ decreased strongly as the $\mathsf{N}$ content of the resulting carbons increased (Figure 3b,c).[6,10] For the interlayer d-spacing, such a strong correlation could be attributed to increased van der Waals interactions that encourage tighter packing (i.e., smaller $\mathrm{d}_{002}$ ) of the more-polarizable, N-rich carbon nanoplatelets. Likewise, the decrease in the $L_{\mathrm{a}}$ parameter (and slight increase in ${\bf d}_{101}$ ) with N content can be explained in terms of defects; the most N-rich carbons synthesized in this study displayed about $1\mathrm{~N~}$ defect for every 3 C atoms in the material, indicating a highly-defective structure with significant disruption to the in-plane order as compared to pure graphite/graphene. This analysis demonstrates that, during molten salt synthesis, the final N content, which is primarily controlled by the choice of precursor and the synthetic temperature, plays a strong role in determining the fringe parameters of these turbostratic carbons. On the other hand, molten salt templating enables decoupling of surface N content from the porosity and/or surface area.
## 2.2. Quantifying Selective $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Adsorption
Following quantification of the surface chemistry, porosity, and semi-crystallinity of these carbons, analysis of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isotherms acquired at various temperatures revealed the thermodynamics of adsorption (Figure 4 and Figures S32–S54, Supporting Information). Following our previous works, a temperature- and pressure-dependent dual-site Langmuir isotherm model was fit to the observed isotherm data via multiple regression in OriginPro.[6,7] This isotherm model considers $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption into two separate adsorption sites that exhibit unique adsorption energetics, such that one “strong” site fills before (i.e., at lower partial pressures than) a site exhibiting smaller relative adsorption affinity (i.e., the “weak” site). In the dual-site Langmuir model, each distinct adsorption site utilizes three adjustable parameters, describing the site capacity $(q_{\mathrm{i,sat}})$ and the thermodynamics (i.e., differential enthalpy $(\Delta H_{\mathrm{i}})$ and entropy $(\Delta S_{\mathrm{i}}))$ of adsorption. With these thermodynamic factors, the temperature-dependent Langmuir coefficients are derived via the Van’t Hoff relation. As a result, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption behavior can be predicted over a wide range of temperatures and pressures by specifying six unique isotherm parameters. This isotherm model is summarized in Equations (S2) and (S3), Supporting Information.
Our previous works related the “strong” adsorption site, dubbed as the “A” site, to the interlayer void space in this class of semi-crystalline carbons.[6,7] In this adsorption site, the isosteric heat of adsorption was shown to be tunable from ${\approx}30$ to $50~\mathrm{kJ}$ $\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ via manipulation of N content and interlayer d-spacing. Physically, a decreased interlayer spacing is thought to induce favorable enthalpic interactions due to confinement by enabling a stronger coupling of the adsorption potentials from opposing pore walls (i.e., carbon nanoplatelets). On the other hand, the weaker adsorption site, dubbed site “B,” was assigned to the filling of larger micropores (diameters ${<}2~\mathrm{nm}$ ) in the carbon materials. The weaker site (associated with pore filling) typically exhibited heats of adsorption ${\approx}10\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ smaller than observed in the strong site (associated with intercalative-type adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ within the semi-crystalline nanostructure). As these larger micropores are accessible to both $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ molecules, sizesieving selectivity is associated primarily with adsorption in the strong, “A” site, especially for dilute streams where significant adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from high temperatures or low partial pressures is required.
The saturation capacities $(q_{\mathrm{i,sat}})$ were variable across the dataset, ranging from ${\approx}0.3$ to $0.9\ \mathrm{mmol\g^{-1}}$ and from ${\approx}1.6$ to 3.2 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for the strong (A) and weak (B) sites, respectively (Figure 4a,c). These turbostratic carbons exhibit moderate capacity for the strong site but relatively small capacity for the weak site compared to other highly-microporous carbons with surface areas surpassing $2000\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .[41] For the strong site, the capacity seemed to increase with the $\mathrm{~N~}$ content of the precursor and the salt:carbon ratio, and the largest saturation capacities were achieved at intermediate synthetic temperatures near $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Carbons synthesized from N-poor precursors, with small salt:carbon ratios, and at high temperatures exhibited the lowest capacities of this strong site. On the other hand, the capacity of the weaker adsorption site seemed to increase strongly with synthesis temperature and slightly with both the N content and the salt:carbon ratio of the precursors.
For adsorption affinity (Figure 4b,d), the strong site exhibited $\Delta G_{\mathrm{ads}}$ ranging from approximately $-15.5$ to $-19\mathrm{k}$ J $\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The affinity of the weak site was typically ${\approx}8.5\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ weaker than observed for the strong site and spanned a range of approximately $-7.5{-}10\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . For both the strong and the weak sites, the synthesis temperature and the N content of the precursor seemed to exert the most significant influences on $\Delta G_{\mathrm{ads}}$ , as low synthetic temperatures and N-rich precursors were generally associated with increased affinity.
As a net result, the capacity of the strong site $(q_{\mathrm{A,~sat}})$ was largely uncorrelated to the capacity of the weak site $(q_{\mathrm{B,\thinspacesat}})$ . This is visualized graphically in Figure 5a and importantly suggests that these disparate adsorption mechanisms could be tuned orthogonally for adsorbent optimization. In contrast, across both strong and weak sites, the adsorption affinity $(\Delta G_{\mathrm{i}})$ seemed to follow similar trends with respect to the synthesis variables; in general, a sample with large relative affinity in the strong adsorption site $(-\Delta G_{\mathrm{A}})$ also displayed relatively large affinity in the weaker adsorption site $(-\Delta G_{\mathrm{B}})$ (Figure 5b).
Additionally, Figures S32–S54, Supporting Information, exhibit the differential isosteric enthalpy, entropy, and free energy of adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ for these carbons. Good fits between the numerically and analytically-derived thermodynamic quantities further support the validity of the dual-site Langmuir model to adequately describe the physics of adsorption for this class of materials. In agreement with our previous works, an apparent linear free energy relation was observed between the entropy and the enthalpy of adsorption in both adsorption sites (Figure 5c,d). We previously related this phenomenon to synergy in the impact of confinement and surface N content on configurational entropy and adsorption enthalpy.[6,42,43] In confining adsorption sites, stronger van der Waals interactions increase enthalpic adsorbentadsorbate stabilization but also hinder translational and/or rotational degrees of freedom (i.e., entropy) of the adsorbate compared to the free gas phase. Slight deviations from this linear exchange relation were induced by increasing the N content, which conceivably strengthened the enthalpy of adsorption due to enhanced dipole–dipole interactions between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and the polarizable N-rich surface while exhibiting a smaller relative effect on configurational entropy, which is dictated largely by sterics of adsorption site geometry.
In sum, in both the strong and the weak sites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, a strong linear free energy relation between the enthalpy and entropy of adsorption was observed. The net affinity $(-\Delta G_{\mathrm{i}})$ increased with precursor N content and decreased with synthetic temperature. However, the capacities of the two adsorption sites were largely uncorrelated; the capacity of the weak site $(q_{\mathrm{B,\thinspacesat}})$ increased with temperature and $\mathrm{~N~}$ content, but the capacity of the strong site $(q_{\mathrm{A,~sat}})$ was maximized at intermediate synthetic temperatures for N-rich precursors with large salt ratios.
In addition to strong $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption at the partial pressures and temperatures of interest for post-combustion flue gas, an adsorbent effective for carbon capture must also exhibit limited adsorption of molecular $\Nu_{2}$ to achieve sufficient adsorption selectivity. In order to probe the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity, pure component $\Nu_{2}$ isotherms at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figures S55–S57, Supporting Information) were fit to the Henry’s isotherm model to enable the application of the ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST). The Henry’s constants at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(k_{\mathrm{N}_{2}})$ for the various turbostratic carbons displayed in Figure 6a, show a strong dependence on the synthesis temperature and N content of the precursors; higher temperature treatment of N-poor precursors resulted in the largest $\Nu_{2}$ uptakes at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
Utilizing the six dual-site Langmuir parameters and the Henry’s constants describing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption, respectively, the selectivity of adsorption was predicted by IAST via the open-source Python package pyIAST.[44] Employing a total pressure of 1 atm and temperature of $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , binary isotherms were simulated by varying the molar ratio of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the gas phase (Figures S58 and S59, Supporting Information). From these binary isotherms, the selectivity at a feed composition of $4\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (such as those encountered in the natural gas combined cycle [NGCC] flue gases) was quantified with Equation (S7), Supporting Information. As shown in Figure 6b, the selectivity was highly variable within the dataset, spanning the range from ${\approx}50$ to 330, and increased with the weight percent of melamine used in the precursor, decreased strongly with temperature, and was maximized at intermediate salt:carbon ratios. These values of selectivity are exceptional for porous carbons, which routinely exhibit selectivity near 20, and approach or surpass the performance of leading metal-organic frameworks (Table S12 and Figures S67 and S68, Supporting Information). Increased selectivity corresponds to a larger purity of captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , and for a binary feed stream of $4\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , a selectivity greater than 216 is required for purities $590\%$ in the adsorbed phase. For the set of carbons synthesized in this study, the predicted adsorbate phase purities achieved from a feed of $4\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ spanned from $\approx70\%$ to $93\%$ between the least and most selective samples (Figure S60,
Supporting Information). Only the carbons derived from the most N-rich precursors and synthesized at relatively low temperatures achieved values of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity sufficient for efficient separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from the typically dilute post-combustion flue gas. This exceptional performance, which is on par even with some chemisorbing metal-organic frameworks, is attributed to the unique size-sieving selectivity enabled by the semi-crystalline turbostratic nanostructure of these carbons, which exhibit only moderate $\Nu_{2}$ -accessible surface areas but a large quantity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ - accessible ultramicroporosity.
## 2.3. Correlating Adsorbent Properties with Performance
The similarity matrix shown in Figure 7 reveals further insights into latent structure-function relationships between material properties and selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption performance of these nanoporous carbon adsorbents. The top left quadrant of Figure 7 further demonstrates that 1) the BET surface area and the microporosity are highly correlated and 2) the $\mathrm{~N~}$ content is intimately related to the observed semi-crystalline fringe parameters. However, these two sets of related material properties (i.e., microporosity and BET surface area vs N content and turbostratic fringe parameters) are uncorrelated with respect to each other, further demonstrating that molten salt templating enables decoupling of the surface area and chemistry. In other words, tuning N content induces changes to the turbostratic nanostructure, but the $(\Nu_{2}$ -accessible) nanoporosity can be manipulated independently from these parameters.
The bottom right quadrant of the similarity matrix in Figure 7 shows the correlation between the parameters describing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ adsorption and the selectivity as predicted by the IAST. The saturation capacity of the strong site $(q_{\mathrm{A,~sat}})$ is largely independent of the other adsorption parameters and is only loosely correlated with the affinity of the strong site $(-\Delta G_{\mathrm{A}})$ . On the other hand, the affinity of the strong site $(-\Delta G_{\mathrm{A}})$ is strongly correlated with the affinity of the weak site $(-\Delta G_{\mathrm{B}})$ and with the predicted selectivity at a feed of $4\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(\ensuremath{\mathsf{S}}_{4\%})$ . In fact, of all the parameters describing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption, the selectivity displayed the most significant correlation with this affinity of the strong site, $-\Delta G_{\mathrm{A}}$ .
For the weak adsorption site, the saturation capacity $(q_{\mathrm{B,\mathrm{sat}}})$ seemed negatively correlated with the sorbent affinity $(-\Delta G_{\mathrm{A,B}})$ and the predicted selectivity $\ensuremath{(\mathrm{S}_{4\%})}$ . The Henry’s constants for $\Nu_{2}$ at $30\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ (k_{\mathrm{N}_{2}})$ were uncorrelated to the strong site capacities $(q_{\mathrm{A,~sat}})$ but positively correlated to the capacities of the weak adsorption site $(q_{\mathrm{B,\thinspacesat}})$ , where competitive adsorption is hypothesized to occur. As expected, selectivity decreased as the Henry’s constant for $\Nu_{2}$ increased. Interestingly, $\Nu_{2}$ uptake seemed negatively correlated with the free energy for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, especially in the strong adsorption site $(-\Delta G_{\mathrm{A}})$ .
The remaining two quadrants of the matrix in Figure 7 depict the correlations between material properties and selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption performance. Of all the parameters describing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, only the capacity of the weak, non-selective adsorption site $(q_{\mathrm{B,\mathrm{sat}}})$ was strongly correlated with the porosity and/or surface area. In contrast, the N content and turbostratic nanostructure were both strongly correlated to the affinity of the strong adsorption site $(-\Delta G_{\mathrm{A}})$ and the resulting $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity $\ensuremath{(\mathrm{S}_{4\%})}$ . Interestingly, Henry’s constants for $\Nu_{2}$ at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were negatively correlated to the N content, likely as an indirect result of size-sieving enabled by the smaller interlayer d-spacings incurred with larger N content. Fundamentally, by manipulating the interlayer d-spacing and the linear free energy relationship that dictates the affinity for physisorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , increased $\mathrm{~N~}$ contents were associated with more $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (but less $\Nu_{2}$ ) uptakes, even from dilute feeds, and resulted in increased IAST-predicted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity.
## 2.4. Developing, Visualizing, and Validating Response Surface Models
Informed from this quantification of material properties and selective adsorption performance for the set of turbostratic carbons in this design of experiments study, empirical mapping of various responses (e.g., material properties and adsorption performance) was enabled via step-wise multiple regression (Section S2, Supporting Information). Reduced second-order models with interactions between the four synthetic variables of interest (weight percent of melamine in precursor, synthetic temperature, salt:carbon ratio, and cooling rate) were employed.[45] More complex models, such as those enabled by machine learning via support vector regression with radial basis function kernels, come at the risk of overfitting and could incur less generalizability for relatively small datasets with limited degrees of freedom, as is the case for the present study. The second-order model enables a rudimentary method for capturing curvature (i.e., deviations from linearity) by including a quadratic term for each of the four unique synthetic variables. Additionally, interaction terms between two synthesis variables quantify how the effect of one variable depends on the value of a different variable. If a given model displays significant interactions between two independent variables I and II, then the effect of variable I on a given observed response also depends on the value of variable II, and vice versa.
The final N content followed different trends than the surface area/porosity, as noted previously. As visible in Figure 8, the N content decreased most significantly with the synthesis temperature. The resulting N content also showed a weaker, quadratic dependence on the salt:carbon ratio; increased slightly with the N content of the precursor; and exhibited a slight interaction between the salt:carbon ratio and the cooling rate (“saltcool”). Thus, Figure 8 summarizes how each synthesis variable (N content of precursor, temperature, salt:carbon ratio, and cooling rate), as well as their interactions, dictate the resulting response in material properties and/or performance for selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption. In general, the synthesis temperature (“temp”) was observed to significantly impact nearly all the properties of the resulting carbons, and the N content of the precursor (“smx”) played a secondary effect. The salt:carbon ratio (“salt”) served to further modulate the responses, and the cooling rate (“cool”) enabled even finer-tuning of carbon adsorbent properties.
For the models of the dual-site Langmuir parameters describing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, the terms describing interactions between synthetic variables were observed to exhibit significant effects. For example, while lower temperatures were generally associated with increased adsorption affinity in both the strong and weak adsorption sites, the interactions of the precursor N content with the synthesis temperature (“smx-temp”) and the salt:carbon ratio with the cooling rate (“salt-cool”) were both observed to strongly impact the resulting free energies of adsorption. Hence, the relationship between adsorbent affinity and the synthesis temperature was dependent on the N content of the precursors. For N-rich precursors, lower synthesis temperatures resulted in larger adsorbent affinity. However, the reverse was true for Npoor precursors, where higher synthesis temperatures were predicted to increase the affinity for adsorption. Further, the dependence of adsorption affinity on the cooling rate was affected by the salt:carbon ratio. Precursor mixtures with large salt:carbon ratios exhibited the largest affinities when the slowest cooling rates were applied; but precursors with small salt:carbon ratios were predicted to achieve larger affinity when fast cooling rates were employed. As shown in Figure 8, adsorption affinity was significantly impacted by nearly all of these complex interactions between the synthesis variables. For example, of all the terms describing possible pairwise interactions, only the interaction of precursor N content with the cooling rate (“smx-cool”) exhibited a negligible influence on the free energy of adsorption in the strong site $(-\Delta G_{\mathrm{A}})$ . The notable impact of these interaction effects on the models for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption affinity points toward the merit of this design of experiments methodology in optimizing adsorbent synthesis; one-variable-at-at-time studies often fail to identify such important covariant interaction effects.
Alternatively, molecular $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ uptakes, as quantified by Henry’s coefficients at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , were predicted to increase strongly with 1) the interaction of precursor $\mathrm{~N~}$ content with the salt ratio (“smx-salt”) and 2) the synthesis temperature. The Henry’s coefficients for $\Nu_{2}$ also slightly increased with the N content of the precursor and decreased with the salt:carbon ratio. As a result, though $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption was only weakly dependent on the main (i.e., non-interacting) effect of salt:carbon ratio, the selectivity of adsorption—being dependent both on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption— was strongly influenced by temperature, the quadratic effect of salt:carbon ratio, and the interaction of precursor $\mathsf{N}$ content with synthetic temperature. In other words, selectivity was maximized at low synthesis temperatures, with N-rich precursors, and at intermediate salt:carbon ratios. This combination of synthesis parameters resulted in semi-crystalline, N-rich carbons with a good balance of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity in the strong site but with diminished $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption due to relatively small $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ -accessible) surface areas.
Comparing the models for N content and surface area illustrated in Figure 9a,b clearly demonstrates that this molten salt synthetic technique enables decoupling of surface chemistry and $(\Nu_{2}$ -accessible) porosity. A wide range of carbons are predicted to exhibit near-identical N contents with significantly different surface areas, and vice versa. For example, these response surface models predict that a range of carbons could be synthesized with BET surface areas of ${\approx}200\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ but N contents ranging from 10 to $25\mathrm{at\%}$ by judicious choice of synthesis variables. As previously discussed, the N content was most sensitive to the synthesis temperature and the precursor $\mathrm{~N~}$ content, such that N-rich precursors synthesized at lower temperatures exhibited the largest predicted $\mathrm{~N~}$ contents $(>27\ \mathrm{at\%})$ (Figure 9a). On the other hand, the surface area, which varied an order of magnitude from ${\approx}50$ to $500\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , was minimized at low temperatures, regardless of precursor identity. However, the magnitude of the increase in surface area at higher synthesis temperatures was dependent both on the precursor $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ content and the salt:carbon ratio (Figure 9b). Precursors with small $\mathrm{~N~}$ contents were predicted to exhibit increased surface areas only with small salt:carbon ratios. On the other hand, for N-rich precursors, intermediate synthetic temperatures and large salt:carbon ratios were predicted to result in the largest surface areas.
This difference in the production of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ -accessible) porosity and surface area could be attributed to differences in the solubility or strength of interaction between the carbon precursors and the molten eutectic NaCl/KCl due to changes in the polarizability (i.e., N content) of the carbon precursors. In this way, molten salt synthesis of nanoporous carbons can be considered analogous to traditional sol-gel chemistry, likely proceeding through colloidal dispersion and stabilization of partially-condensed oligomers before complete carbonization.[14,15,18] Changes to the stability (and size) of the carbon-salt interface (i.e., surface energy) can induce phase separation and modulate the resulting porosity via hard templating. Additionally, molten salt synthesis could also encourage a “self-activation” mechanism, wherein reactive gases emitted during pyrolysis can create additional nanoporosity.
Our previous work demonstrated that both the pyrolytic decomposition pathway and the initial nanostructure are strongly dependent on the N content of carbon precursors.[10] Precursors with larger initial $\mathrm{~N~}$ contents exhibit increased crystalline order and bear melem-type functionalities, and pyrolysis proceeds mainly through the evolution of $\Nu_{2}$ , HCN, and NCCN species. The observed decrease of N content in the resulting carbons with increased synthetic temperature is attributed to the offgassing of these N-containing species during synthesis. In contrast, pyrolysis of precursors with small N content (but increased O species) results in the evolution of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , CO, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gases at lower relative temperatures. As a result, changes to the precursor N content could modulate the temperature-dependence and speciation of pyrolysis gases, which induce porosity via physical self-activation.[15] Due to the relatively high melting point of the NaCl/KCl eutectic $(657~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ compared to other common eutectic salt mixtures and the semi-carbonized nature of the N-containing carbon precursors employed, this self-activation mechanism, in combination with nanostructural reorganization of semi-crystallinity mediated by the liquid reaction media at elevated temperature, is likely the predominant mechanism of porogenesis in the present study.
As shown in Figure $\mathfrak{s}_{\mathtt{C}}$ (and in agreement with previous analyses), the interlayer d-spacing was predicted to trend inversely with the final N content, ostensibly due to changes in van der Waals interactions between individual graphenic domains as polarizability (i.e., N content) is increased. As such, the smallest d-spacings were predicted to occur for N-rich precursors pyrolyzed at temperatures below ${\approx}800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . For these N-rich precursors, the d-spacing was predicted to vary slightly with salt:carbon ratio, where larger salt ratios resulted in enlarged interlayer spacing. However, for the N-poor precursors, the synthesis temperature was predicted to impart the most significant influence; the salt ratio only marginally impacted the predicted d-spacing for N-poor precursors. Samples resulting in the lowest predicted $\mathrm{~N~}$ content (i.e., synthesized at temperatures near ${\approx}900^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ were also predicted to exhibit the largest interlayer d-spacing.
The capacity $(q_{\mathrm{A,~sat}})$ and affinity $(-\Delta G_{\mathrm{A}})$ of the strong adsorption site that determines $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake at the low relative pressures of interest for post-combustion carbon capture are depicted visually in Figure 9d,e. Overall, the capacity of this strong adsorption site was maximized at intermediate temperatures, regardless of the composition of the precursors. For precursors with relatively small N content, smaller salt:carbon ratios were associated with increased adsorption capacity. However, the capacity was maximized for N-rich precursors with large salt:carbon ratios. Carbons synthesized at the highest temperatures exhibited a decreased capacity for this high-affinity adsorption site. The free energy of adsorption in the strong site $(-\Delta G_{\mathrm{A}})$ was predicted to vary similarly with the composition of the precursor (i.e., salt:carbon ratio and N content) as did the strong site capacity $(q_{\mathrm{A,~sat}})$ . However, although $q_{\mathrm{A}}$ , sat was maximized at intermediate synthesis temperatures, the magnitude of $-\Delta G_{\mathrm{A}}$ was largest at the lowest synthesis temperatures. This slight decoupling of adsorption affinity from capacity likely reflects relative changes in the turbostratic nanostructure (associated more with capacity) and the surface chemistry (associated more with affinity). As $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption depends on both capacity and affinity, the carbons predicted to exhibit the largest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption at the necessary relative pressures were synthesized from N-rich precursors, with large salt:carbon ratios, and at synthesis temperatures near $750^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
Ample $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption is a necessary, but not sufficient, property of an adsorbent for carbon capture. The selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption is instead perhaps the most important property required to enable the separation process and has historically limited the applicability of nanoporous carbon adsorbents. As aforementioned, an IAST-selectivity value of at least 216 from a feed of $4\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in $\Nu_{2}$ (representative of NGCC flue gas) is required to obtain a target $90\%$ purity of the adsorbed phase. As shown in Figure 9e, this range of selectivity is only achieved by a subset of all the possible turbostratic carbons. This “hot spot” of synthesis parameters that result in sufficiently-selective carbons required the largest precursor N contents, the lowest synthesis temperatures, and intermediate salt:carbon ratios. The predicted model values for selectivity decreased significantly as the synthesis temperature increased and as the N content of the precursors decreased.
These developed surface response models serve to interpolate between the observations of the original set of carbon synthesized in this DoE study, both to understand the synthetic mechanism and to predict the properties of carbons resulting from combinations of synthesis variables not originally employed. Subsequently, a set of additional nanoporous carbon adsorbents (dubbed samples $^{\mathfrak{a}}\mathrm{A}^{\prime\prime}\mathrm{-}^{\mathfrak{a}}\mathrm{G}^{\prime\prime})$ was created with unique, previously “unseen” combinations of synthetic variables to 1) validate the derived response surface models and 2) attempt to optimize adsorbent properties. For three of these carbons $(^{\omega}\mathrm{A},^{\prime\prime}^{\omega}\mathrm{B},^{\prime\prime}\mathrm{C}^{\prime\prime})$ , the synthesis variables of interest were randomly generated, given only the constraint that the Euclidean distance from the origin (center point) of the combination of synthetic variables was less than the maximum in the original dataset (coded value of 1.0). In other words, these combinations of synthesis variables were allowed to vary randomly within the 4D region of model validity, where the interpolative accuracy of the Doehlert design is expected. On the other hand, the synthesis conditions for two of these carbons ( $\ensuremath{{^\circ}}\mathrm{D}^{\prime\prime}$ and “E”) were not randomly chosen but were instead identified from local optima in the response surface models, effectively acting as “champion” materials. The synthesis variables for sample D were identified as the combination of precursor N content, salt:carbon ratio, synthetic temperature, and cooling rate within the region of model validity that enabled the largest uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from a binary gas feed of $4\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in $\Nu_{2}$ at 1 atm and $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ given the additional constraint of at least $90\%$ purity in the adsorbed phase. In other words, sample D was predicted to exhibit the largest quantity of sufficiently-selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake accessible in the design space. Likewise, sample E was predicted to exhibit the largest achievable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity in the region of model validity (with no constraint related to the quantity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorbed). Two additional samples (“F” and “G”) served to examine the capability of the developed models to predict samples with large selectivity $(^{\mathfrak{a}}\mathrm{F}^{\mathfrak{\eta}})$ and large, selective uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(^{\omega}\mathrm{G}^{\gamma})$ outside the 4D region originally investigated with the DoE dataset.
In sum, samples A–C served to probe model accuracy; samples D and E were identified as promising, “champion” adsorbents via inverse design; and samples F and G served to examine the accuracy of extrapolations of the developed surface response models outside the investigated synthetic design space. A table summarizing the exact synthesis variables employed, as well as selected properties predicted by the various surface response models, can be found in Table S7, Supporting Information.
After the synthesis of the new validation set of nanoporous carbons, analogous characterization of material properties and screening of adsorption performance were conducted, and the results are summarized in Figure 10. Additional parity plots, which are useful to check against model overfitting, are also provided in Figures S63 and S64, Supporting Information. For the subset of samples with synthesis parameters falling within the region of expected model validity (Euclidean distance $<1.0$ from the central point, samples $^{\mathfrak{a}}\mathrm{A}^{\prime\prime}\mathrm{-}^{\mathfrak{a}}\mathrm{E}^{\prime\prime})$ , the material properties and adsorption performance indicators quantitatively fall within the $95\%$ confidence intervals predicted by the response surface models for nearly all observations. Impressively, for samples A–C, the complete $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ isotherms at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for pressures up to 760 Torr were successfully predicted quantitively from the synthesis variables alone by applying the various surface response models of site capacities/affinities and Henry’s constant to the dual-site Langmuir and Henry’s isotherm models for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption, respectively (Figure 10g,h). The slight disagreement at larger relative pressures for samples D and E is attributed to a smaller-than-predicted capacity of the weak adsorption site, likely due to a smaller-than-predicted microporous surface area (Figure 10a).
Even for metrics that fell outside the predicted confidence intervals, the models accurately capture qualitative trends among the validation set, suggesting that general insights into the synthesis process and structure-function relationships can be extracted from the response surface models developed in this study. Please note: due to changes in the configuration of the instrument used for XPS analysis over the course of this study, the systematic disagreement in predicted versus observed N contents (Figure 10b and Figure S63, Supporting Information) could be attributed to unavoidable systematic errors. Notably, the extrapolated samples (“F” and “G”) exhibited worse agreement between the model predictions and the true observed values in general. This result serves as an important caution toward overinterpreting results from DoE studies; confidence in the quantitative predictions of response surface models should only be extended so far as the design space probed by the original training dataset.
Analyzing the (dis)agreement between model predictions and observed responses reveals further insight. For example, samples D, F, and G all exhibited decreased surface areas (Figure 10a), N contents (Figure 10b), and interlayer spacing (Figure 10c), but increased stacking coherence ( $\mathrm{\Delta}L_{\mathrm{c}}$ Figure 10d) compared with the range predicted by the response surface models. These samples were also all synthesized using the precursors with the largest N contents examined in this study (i.e., $60\mathrm{\wt\%}$ melamine, or $^{\omega}\mathrm{SM}60^{\prime\prime})$ . The worse prediction accuracy for this subset is perhaps unsurprising. The original DoE dataset contained only one sample synthesized with this $\mathrm{~N~}$ content in the precursor, and the models’ predictive accuracy would be expected to scale with the density of experimental observations in the training dataset. Likewise, for selectivity (Figure 10j), quantitative agreement was achieved for all samples except those synthesized at the lowest temperature of $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (“E,” “F,” and $^{\omega}\mathbf{G}^{\prime\prime}$ ). The increased selectivity observed (as compared to the model predictions) is largely attributed to a drastically overpredicted $\Nu_{2}$ uptake (Figure 10h). This analysis suggests that the predictive accuracy of the models decays as the synthesis variables approach the extreme values investigated in this study.
However, despite disagreement between the observed and predicted selectivity, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity of ${\approx}1080$ obtained by sample G was extraordinary. This performance corresponds to a nearly $25\times$ increase in the concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from $4\%$ in the gas phase to an adsorbed phase purity of $\approx98\%$ . Again, this impressive IAST selectivity is attributed to a modest $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity $(\approx0.46\mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1}})$ alongside near-negligible $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ uptakes $({\approx}0.018\ \mathrm{mmol\g^{-1}},$ ) at the gas feed composition of interest $(4\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $96\%\mathrm{N}_{2}$ at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 1 atm). In fact, sample G was predicted to achieve greater than $90\%$ adsorbed phase purity from ultradilute streams with gas-phase $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentrations as low as $0.5\%$ though the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity at these conditions was predicted to be quite low $({\approx}0.13\ \mathrm{mmol\g^{-1}})$ (Figures S67 and S69, Supporting Information). This performance in selectivity (albeit with lower working capacity) is comparable to state-of-the-art, chemisorbing metal-organic frameworks (Figures S67 and S68, Supporting Information).[46,47] As such, the outstanding performance of sample G further exemplifies the merit of limiting $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptake by these size-sieving carbon physisorbents to enable highly selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption from dilute streams.
Comparing the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption isotherms of samples A $(\approx14\ \mathrm{at\%}$ N, $\approx370{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}.$ ) and G $(\approx24~\mathrm{at\%}$ , ${\approx}70{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1},$ ) further emphasizes the importance of minimizing $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption by limiting the BET surface area for this class of adsorbents (Figure S65, Supporting Information). Although samples A and G both achieve a near-identical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity of ${\approx}0.46$ mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at a pressure of 30.4 Torr $(0.04\mathrm{atm})$ , which corresponds to the typical partial pressure of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in NGCC flue gas, the uptakes of $\Nu_{2}$ at 729.6 Torr (0.96 atm) by these carbons were significantly different. Sample A exhibited an $\Nu_{2}$ capacity of ${\approx}0.15\ \mathrm{mmol\g^{-1}}$ , which was nearly an order of magnitude larger than the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ capacity of sample G $({\approx}0.018\ \mathrm{mmol\g^{-1}},$ ) at identical conditions. As a result, the IAST-predicted adsorbed phase purity of sample A $(\approx80\%\mathrm{CO}_{2})$ ) was much smaller than that achieved by the highly selective sample G $({\approx}98\%\mathrm{CO}_{2})$ ). This more than $10\times$ increase in IAST selectivity (96 vs 1083) can clearly be attributed to the variable $\Nu_{2}$ uptakes, which are dependent on the ${\bf N}_{2}$ -accessible surface areas.
In sum, the important results from this analysis of the validation set of molten salt-derived carbons show that the response surface models developed in this study enable the inverse design of nanoporous carbons with tailored physical/chemical properties and selective adsorption performance. For example, a “champion” material (sample D) achieves sufficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity $(>90\%$ purity) with a working capacity of ${\approx}0.73$ mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under the simulated application in post-combustion capture from dry NGCC flue gas $(4\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 1 atm). This performance surpasses nearly all previously reported nanoporous carbon physisorbents, to the best of our knowledge. While other carbons have achieved increased $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacities, especially at larger partial pressures, very few have simultaneously exhibited sufficient selectivity for application with such dilute gas streams, largely due to their increased $\mathbf{\bar{N}}_{2}$ -accessible) surface areas and concurrent deleterious $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption.
## 2.5. Understanding General Structure-Function Relations
With sufficient confidence in the accuracy of the developed response surface models, relationships between the predicted adsorption performance indicators and material properties were analyzed to extract latent structure-function relationships. During the production of the heat maps shown in Figure 9, the properties of all synthetically accessible carbon materials were predicted using the developed response surface models. Given the reasonable predictive accuracy of the various response surface models, this artificial population of accessible carbons can then feasibly be analyzed to uncover relationships between adsorbent structure and performance for selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption (Figure 11).
Histograms shown in Figure 11a reveal the distribution of material properties accessible within the synthetic design space for which the response surface models are expected to have reasonable predictive ability. The subset of samples shown in orange emphasizes the apparent material properties of samples with selectivity sufficient to obtain at least $90\%$ purity from a binary feed stream of $4\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in $\Nu_{2}$ at $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Notably, samples were predicted to obtain sufficient adsorptive selectivity only with the lowest surface areas $(<300~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and largest $\mathrm{~N~}$ contents $(>21\ \mathrm{at\%})$ . As aforementioned, this increase in N content was also reflected in the semi-crystalline fringe parameters describing the turbostratic nanostructure of these carbons. These N-rich carbons were also predicted to display the lowest interlayer spacing $(\mathrm{d}_{002},<3.47\mathring\mathrm{A})$ , large stacking coherence $(L_{\mathrm{c}},>9.5\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ , and poor in-plane order $(\mathrm{d}_{101}^{-},>4.25\ \tilde{\mathring{\mathrm{A}}};\ L_{\mathrm{a}},<5\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ . In terms of key adsorption parameters, this selective subset of carbons was predicted to exhibit the largest magnitudes of free energies of adsorption, in both the strong and weak adsorption sites $(-\Delta G_{\mathrm{A}}>17\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ ; $-\Delta G_{\mathrm{B}}>$ $8.2\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ ). However, for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity, the selective subset of carbons was predicted to exhibit low saturation capacities for the weak adsorption site $(q_{\mathrm{B,sat}}<2.3\ \mathrm{mmol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and only moderate capacity for the strong adsorption site $(0.45\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}<q_{\mathrm{A}}$ , sat $<0.8\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ). Meanwhile, sufficient selectivity was only obtained when Henry’s constants describing $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption were in the range of $\approx0.05{-}0.125\ \mathrm{mmol\g^{-1}}$ atm.
In essence, this analysis suggests that sufficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity is only achieved by carbon adsorbents that exhibit a combination of 1) an N-rich chemistry that helps induce 2) semicrystalline domains of carbon with a tight and coherent interlayer packing but 3) a very disordered and defective in-plane atomic arrangement and 4) relatively limited $\Nu_{2}$ -accessible surface areas. Such an N-rich nanostructure seemingly results in large adsorption affinity and moderate capacities for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in a size-sieving, selective adsorption site but only limited capacity for either $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ or $\Nu_{2}$ in a weaker adsorption site related to microporous surface area.
Given sufficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity (and purity of the adsorbed phase), adsorbents that exhibit larger adsorption capacities at the feed conditions of interest should be expected to improve the overall performance in a carbon capture process by increasing working capacity, throughput, and overall energy efficiency. In terms of the dual-site Langmuir model used here to parameterize $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption by this class of carbons, an ideal adsorbent would exhibit large capacities for the strong adsorption site $(q_{\mathrm{A,\thinspacesat}})$ while exhibiting sufficient IAST selectivity $_{(>216)}$ . Accordingly, Figure $11\mathrm{b-g}$ exhibits how characteristic material properties (i.e., BET surface area and N content) relate to the various adsorption performance indicators to aid in further identifying structure-function relationships and perhaps uncover any fundamental limitations of this adsorbent class.
As shown in Figure 11b, the capacity of the strong adsorption site was dependent both on the N content and the surface area. A discontinuity apparent in the heat maps of these figures is related to the parameter describing interlayer stacking coherence; while N content was predicted to trend strongly with the interlayer d-spacing, a wide range of values for the stacking coherence $\left(\boldsymbol{L}_{\mathrm{c}}\right)$ were predicted to be synthetically accessible (Figure S66, Supporting Information). In other words, predictions from the developed response surface models suggest that carbons with identical surface areas and N contents—but different $L_{\mathrm{c}}$ parameters—are synthetically accessible by employing different combinations of synthetic parameters. As a result, N-rich carbons with small interlayer spacing, but large surface areas and stacking coherence $\left(\boldsymbol{L}_{\mathrm{c}}\right)$ , were predicted to exhibit the largest capacities for this strong adsorption site $(q_{\mathrm{A,~sat}})$ . This is a notable result; while the capacity of the non-selective, weak adsorption site in the dual-site Langmuir model had previously been associated with microporosity and surface area of N-rich carbons,[6,27] structural factors that influenced the capacity of the strong adsorption site had remained elusive.
Indeed, Figure 11d,f reveals that both the capacity of the weak adsorption site $(q_{\mathrm{B,\thinspacesat}})$ and Henry’s constants for $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption $(k_{\mathrm{N}_{2}})$ were much less dependent on the $\mathrm{~N~}$ content and instead were predicted to vary more significantly with the BET surface area. The qualitative similarity of the heat maps in Figure 11d,f reveals the less-selective nature of this weaker adsorption site, which does not benefit from size-sieving separation. On the other hand, the free energies of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, in both the strong and weak adsorption sites, were predicted to be dependent mainly on the $\mathrm{~N~}$ content of the carbon adsorbents (Figure 7c,e). As a net result, only the carbons with both N-rich surface chemistries and moderate BET surface areas were predicted to achieve sufficient IAST $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ selectivity values (Figure 11g).
This analysis identifies a fundamental tradeoff between the adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at low pressures and the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity; although increases to the BET surface area of N-rich carbons were predicted to result in increased $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ working capacities (Figure 11b), a concurrent increase in Henry’s constants for $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption (Figure 11f) were predicted to detrimentally decrease the IAST selectivity and adsorbed phase purity (Figure 11g). As a result, sample D—previously identified as a “champion” adsorbent in the validation set of carbons—likely exhibits performance that is near the global optimum accessible in this synthesis scheme $(q_{\mathrm{CO2},4\%}=0.73$ mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ; $S_{\mathrm{IAST,}4\%}=246$ ; $x_{\mathrm{CO}2}=$ $91\%$ ). However, the relaxation of constraints on selectivity and adsorbed phase purity could allow a wider range of carbons with improved working capacities approaching $0.9\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
## 3. Conclusion
Molten salt synthesis provides fine-tuning of the physicochemical properties of nanoporous carbons that exhibit selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption for carbon capture. By employing a comprehensive design of experiments methodology, this study reveals fundamental insights into complex synthesis-structure-function relationships that allow optimization of nanoporous carbons created with molten salt synthesis. The data-driven approach employed here revealed that the BET surface area of nanoporous carbons can be decoupled from the resulting N content due to the mediating influence of molten salt templates during pyrolysis. This unique feature of molten salt synthesis contrasts with traditional chemical activation, where a tradeoff between porosity and surface chemistry is commonly observed. Further, the turbostratic nanostructure was revealed to be strongly coupled with the N content of these porous carbons, with the most N-rich materials exhibiting a tight and coherent packing of defective carbon platelet-like domains with interlayer d-spacings amenable to molecular sieving of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from $\Nu_{2}$ . Empiricallyderived surface response models enabled systematic mapping of the synthetically-accessible phase space for inverse design of nanoporous carbons with promising performance in selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption from dilute gas streams, such as those encountered in NGCC post-combustion flue gas. The results emphasize that, for nanoporous carbons with relatively non-discriminative physisorptive affinity for various adsorbates (i.e., $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ ), size-sieving selectivity is necessary to achieve adsorbed phase purities $590\%$ for utility in industrial carbon capture processes. After validating the developed models of carbon synthesis, a nanoporous carbon adsorbent with both sufficient selectivity and appreciable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity was identified and synthesized via inverse design. Analyzing predictions from the developed models revealed fundamental structure-function relationships and identified potential intrinsic limitations of this adsorbent class; though N-rich carbons exhibit improved low-pressure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacities with increased surface areas, deleterious $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption that accompanies this increase in exposed surface area detrimentally decreases the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ selectivity and resulting adsorbed phase purity. As a result, this study suggests that the performance of N-rich nanoporous carbons is improved when balancing $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ capacities by targeting materials with only moderate BET surface areas. Future studies should investigate other molten salts that might enable improved material properties; real measurements of multicomponent adsorption equilibria to validate the IAST for these heterogeneous adsorbents; the impact of water (co-)adsorption on selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture; the relationships between carbon nanostructure and transient, non-equilibrium adsorption properties (i.e., transport, kinetics); and the applicability of size-sieving carbons for other challenging $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separations. Coupling the inverse design techniques established in this study with modeling and optimization of swing adsorption processes could identify synthesis conditions required to create N-rich carbons with ideal adsorption properties for energy-efficient carbon capture from NGCC flue gas. Further, the synthesis-structure relationships identified in this study could be useful for applying similar nanoporous carbons to various other technologies relevant to the energy transition, such as energy storage and conversion or other types of environmental remediation.
